\title{OPT-H: a table of comparison results for closed values that survives the machine's sessions (the switch closedpairs, off by default)}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\tag{soundness}
\tag{benchmark}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Optimisation package "OPT-H" of the second wave after the first release, 2026-11-25 (12:12 to 15:35 UTC). The lever is proposal 1 of [the report of package OPT-E](rep-OPT-E), with [its insight on the closed-pair table](ins-OMW4): with nothing kept between declarations the machine and gruszka unfold alike, and gruszka's lead in work is what it remembers from one declaration to the next, above all a per-worker table of comparison results for pairs of closed values that survives its sessions. This report is the design of that table for \code{rlvm-machine}, a prototype behind a new switch that is off by default, what the prototype gives in counts and in time, its soundness argument checked against the code paths, the hostile exports written against it with their official verdicts, and what an adversarial review still has to do before the switch could enter a default set. The result is a set of patches against the main tree; nothing was published, no shared jar or distribution was touched, no Oracle GraalVM was used. The switch was not put into the default set.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ul{
\li{\strong{What was built.} A table per worker that keeps the answer of a comparison of two \em{closed} values across the machine's sessions, under names of the two values that do not depend on a session, behind the new switch \code{closedpairs}. The switch is off by default, is not part of \code{all}, and a strict engine never has it. With it off the patch computes exactly what the base computes: every counter of a deterministic run is identical on the eight corpora (section 6).}
\li{\strong{What it gives in work.} One worker, the executable, the eight corpora: 13 \% (Mathlib) to 41 \% (Init) fewer unfoldings, 5.4 to 21.6 \% fewer ticks, 5 to 18 \% fewer records; nothing on reachability, which has 6 thousand closed comparisons. Eight workers with a table each: 4.5 to 15.1 \% fewer ticks, 11 to 28 \% fewer unfoldings. The estimate of package OPT-E, a third of the unfoldings, holds on Init, Std, cedar and navier-stokes-euler and not on cslib (19 \%) and Mathlib (13 \%). In instructions (user space, aarch64, a whole run): 1.3 \% fewer on Mathlib and 2.0 to 6.7 \% fewer on Init, Std, cedar, con-leche, navier-stokes-euler and cslib with eight workers (sections 7.1, 7.2, 7.5).}
\li{\strong{What it gives in time.} Far less than the counts say. The executable, 8 workers, warm passes, three interleaved rounds in one quiet window (section 7.4): geometric mean 0.994 of the base in the pass and 0.991 in CPU, while the same build with the switch off, which computes what the base computes, is at 1.001 and 0.999 with single corpora between 0.969 and 1.027. Resolved: 1.2 to 2.6 \% of the CPU on Init, Std, cedar and con-leche. Not resolved: any gain on navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib (CPU 0.996, 1.002, 0.993), the corpora that decide the metric. The JVM launcher: 0.976 in the pass, 0.997 in CPU, no regression. Peak resident size: 2 to 9 \% more in the executable. The estimate of 4 to 10 \% of the workers' CPU is not met: the comparisons the table answers are short (31 to 54 ticks each), and what it adds, the names and the probes of its own tables, costs time out of proportion to its instructions.}
\li{\strong{The soundness class, examined.} A kept "true" is a derivable equality of two closed, well-typed terms, found by the machine's own procedure in a declaration that was accepted, and read only by a declaration of higher rank: it cannot make the machine accept a statement that is not derivable. It can make the machine accept where the official kernel, whose caches live for one declaration, rejects a derivable statement: the tolerated kind (d), the class of warm sessions. The task's sentences were checked against the code paths (section 4). Two things came out that the estimate did not have: "false" answers are worth nothing here and are not kept by default (they are 0.7 to 3.3 \% of the answers and at most 0.17 points of the ticks, and a kept "false" is the one way the table makes the machine reject what it accepted before); and an answer from the table skips the reductions whose memos can make the official kernel reject a \em{later} comparison, which gives one new accept of the tolerated kind (section 4.1).}
\li{\strong{Hostile exports.} 78 exports written against the table, each with the verdict of the official checker (leanchecker 4.36.0 in Docker): 41 accept, 37 reject. No statement that is not derivable was accepted by any configuration; 11 rows are of the tolerated kind, each with a certificate; ten of them are accepted by the default set of today, with or without the table. Sixteen seeded faults: eleven are caught by a strict row, one changes the machine's own outcome without a wrong verdict, four are reached by the unit tests only (section 5).}
\li{\strong{Gates}, all passed (section 6): with the switch on: the machine's M8 gate (SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, the audit lanes clean, the pipeline 230 of 230); the hostile matrix (12 new switch sets by 1,274 rows, 0 unexpected); \src{scripts/gate.sh} through the executable and through the jars (230 of 230 tests; 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0; 1,274 hostile rows with 0 wrong accepts and 0 wrong rejects; two heavy mutants at the judge's time limit, rejected when run alone), also with a session per declaration, where the table is the machine's only memory; the eight corpora accepted with 8 and 4 workers on both launchers with the base's declines; the invariance suite (15,183 files by 9 schedules, 0 differences); the differential engine (1,579,713 units of the eight corpora, all agree). With the switch off: every counter of the statistics identical to the base on the eight corpora, and the same gates.}
\li{\strong{Verdict.} \strong{The patch: GO} as an experiment that is off: with the switch off it computes what the base computes and costs 0.03 to 0.06 \% of the instructions. \strong{The switch in the default set: NO-GO.} Not for a wrong verdict, there is none in any gate, and the class is the tolerated one of warm sessions; but (1) on the three large corpora it gives no time that a guard window resolves, while it adds a table of 14 MiB per worker on Mathlib, 2 to 9 \% of peak resident size and one more way to an accept of the tolerated kind; (2) the adversarial pass by somebody who does not own the code, which [the rule for a default](dec-QN1O) requires, was not made. Section 8 has what such a pass has to do and what could make the table worth its time.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What gruszka keeps, and what the machine kept}

\p{[The study of gruszka](study-gruszka-pestka) and its source: at the entry of its comparison, after the session's pair cache and a structural walk, two values without a free variable whose pair of head constants was seen among the kept pairs each get a \em{name}, the id of the substituted, unreduced term the value stands for in a hash-consed node table of the worker that outlives its sessions. A pair of closed names that the table holds with the stamp of an earlier declaration is answered from it, "true" or "false". After a comparison that was computed and unfolded at least once the pair is written with the number of the declaration. Nothing is shared between workers. [The report of package OPT-E](rep-OPT-E), section 4.2, counted 144,000 to 305,000 entries and 209,000 to 656,000 hits per corpus with one worker, and 23 to 35 \% more unfoldings without the table.}

\p{The machine had nothing with that lifetime. Its pair table and every memo word end with [the session](design-warm-sessions), after 40 to 70 declarations at the cap of 1 MiB, and only the shared check facts of \code{pchk} survive. The estimate of OPT-E for a table like gruszka's in the machine was a third of its unfoldings and 4 to 10 \% of the workers' CPU, from gruszka's counts. This package built the table and measured it.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The design, question by question}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 The name of a closed value that does not depend on the session}

\p{A value of the machine is a record of one session ([the value model](design-value-model)): its reference means nothing after a reset, and level ids, list ids, substitution ids, overlay term ids and the ids of computed numbers are issued by overlays that are reset with the session or with the worker's context. A name must be built from what is not: declaration rows, the ids of \em{shared} terms (export and generated region), numbers by value, name ids of universe parameters, structure rows and field numbers. The alternative of the task statement, a content hash with a full comparison on a hit, needs the full comparison to walk two values that no longer exist; a hash alone is not an identity. So the name is the id of a node in a hash-consed node table that lives as long as the machine, as in gruszka: equal ids are equal constructions.}

\table{
\tr{\th{value}\th{its name}}
\tr{\td{constant with its level list}\td{\code{CONST}(declaration row, name of the level list)}}
\tr{\td{application of a constant or stuck projection to arguments}\td{\code{APP}(\code{APP}(name of the head, name of argument 1), ..)}}
\tr{\td{sort}\td{\code{SORT}(name of the level)}}
\tr{\td{Nat literal below 2^31; two-word literal below 2^63}\td{by value}}
\tr{\td{k successors of a value that is not a literal}\td{\code{NSUC}(name of the base, k)}}
\tr{\td{stuck projection}\td{\code{PROJ}(name of the structure value, structure row, field)}}
\tr{\td{closure (lambda, Pi, the type of a lambda) and unforced suspension over a shared term}\td{kind, term id, names of the frame's slots in order (a placeholder slot is a constant), name of the level substitution}}
\tr{\td{suspension whose value is known}\td{the name of that value (the machine itself replaces such a suspension by its value)}}
\tr{\td{universe level, level list, level substitution}\td{by structure: zero, successor, max, imax, the \em{name id} of a parameter; the list of bound parameter names and the list of levels}}
\tr{\td{no name: the value takes no part}\td{a free variable or anything that contains one (flag \code{OPEN}); a let variable or a typed binding (a suspension whose type is not the type of its value); a closure or suspension over an overlay term (a decoded proof block, a term made in the session); a far frame; a Nat literal of 2^63 and more (its id is a number store's); a String literal (a pool index)}}
}

\p{Two values with one name are the same construction up to the structure of levels and up to the machine's own evaluation of a suspension, so they are definitionally equal. Naming is a walk of the two values with a cache per session (4,096 direct-mapped cells) and a budget of 256 nodes; it calls no judgment and forces nothing. \strong{Numbers} (one worker, the final build, section 7): a closed comparison that reaches the pass loop is 9 to 19 \% of the comparisons past the session's tables (6 thousand on reachability, 7.1 million on Mathlib). Of the pairs that pass the filter, 3 to 4 \% have a side without a name (10 to 11 \% on reachability, cedar and con-leche). The first reason is a closure over an overlay term, a lambda of the proof block being checked (2,783 pairs on Init, 16,661 on navier-stokes-euler, 27,162 on Mathlib), then a stored big literal or a string (660, 18 and 6,550; on con-leche they come first, 11,011 against 8,345); the rest are values that the session's cache already knew to have no name. A far frame was met 365 times on cslib and 322 times on Mathlib, the budget of 256 nodes ran out 16 times in the eight corpora together, and a typed binding or a let was never met in a closed pair.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.2 When an answer is read}

\p{At the start of the pass loop of \code{CONV}, after identity, literals, the session's pair table, the structural walk of \code{same}, closure congruence and the forcing of suspended sides, that is exactly where the complete procedure would start to work on the pair. Conditions: both sides closed; plain mode (\code{\verb~|ER == 0~}); not inside a re-computation of a debug mode; the pair of head constants is among the kept pairs (one bit of a 64 KiB bit set per worker: a pair that cannot hit costs two flag tests, one cell of 16 bytes written and one bit read); the entry was written by a declaration of \em{smaller rank} than the one being checked. A hit records \code{POS} in the session's pair table, as a computed answer does, so the session answers the pair from then on. Two values with one name are answered "true" without an entry.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.3 When an answer is written}

\p{Only where \code{Conv.rec} records the final answer of a comparison that \em{completed}: a frame that a budget cut removes never gets there ([the completion rule](design-conversion)), so no entry depends on a budget, and a comparison inside a speculative segment that was cut leaves nothing. Further conditions, all tested at that point: both sides closed; plain mode; the comparison was entered through the lookup above in this declaration and used at least 4 ticks since (the counterpart of gruszka's "unfolded at least once": with one tick as the threshold the table is 5 \% larger on Init and 52 \% larger on navier-stokes-euler for the same gain within 0.2 points, section 7.3); the session holds no possibly mode-dependent answer (section 2.7); by default the answer is "true" (section 2.6). The answer goes to a buffer of the declaration and enters the table when the facade reports the declaration \em{accepted} and the declaration did not enter eager reduction. A declaration that is rejected, declined, abandoned for a restart or cancelled leaves nothing, like the session.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.4 What is keyed}

\p{The unordered pair of the two names, as in the session's pair table; the level instantiation is inside the names (the level list of every constant, the level substitution of every closure, parameters by name), so a pair that is equal under one instantiation has another key under another. The entry is the rank of the declaration that wrote it and the answer. An entry with rank \em{r} is read only by a declaration of rank above \em{r}: everything that was visible when the answer was derived is visible to the reader, whatever order a worker takes declarations in.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.5 Per worker or shared, how large, what a miss costs}

\p{\strong{Per worker}, in the prototype: the table is a field of the machine, so there is one per worker and per pass, no lock, and nothing of one pass or one store is seen by another. What invariance requires of a shared table was thought through and not built: only "true" answers of accepted declarations could be shared (a "true" is a fact about two closed terms and holds for every reader of higher rank; a "false" is the failure of one procedure in one cache state), the rank rule must stay, and the names must be shared with the pairs, which makes the node table a concurrent structure or the lookup a find-only walk of a published snapshot. Which hits a worker gets would then depend on timing, as it already does on the assignment of units to workers; the verdict would differ only where it can differ today, on inputs of the tolerated kind (section 4). \strong{What sharing could add at most} is the distance between one worker and eight private tables: with one worker, one table for the whole file, the ticks go down by 21.6 \% on Init, 17.7 \% on Std, 14.3 \% on cedar, 13.9 \% on con-leche, 12.6 \% on navier-stokes-euler, 7.9 \% on cslib and 5.4 \% on Mathlib (section 7.1); with eight workers and eight tables by 15.1, 12.5, 10.1, 10.1, 9.8, 5.8 and 4.5 \% (section 7.2). The eight tables answer 83 to 92 \% as many comparisons as the one, but together they hold 1.6 to 2 times its pairs: a worker must compute a pair itself before it can answer it, and that first computation is what a shared table would save. So a shared table is worth at most 1 to 6.5 points of the ticks, most on the small corpora, before the cost of a concurrent node table.}

\p{\strong{Size.} A node is 16 bytes plus 8 bytes of index; a pair 16 bytes at a load of one half. Bounds: 2^20 nodes and 2^19 pairs per worker; beyond either the table is emptied at the next declaration start (gruszka: 2^21 nodes). One worker (section 7.1): 1.6 MiB on reachability, 4 to 14 MiB on Init, Std, con-leche and cedar, 28 to 32 MiB on navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib; on Mathlib the bound of 2^20 nodes is reached once and the table starts again (303,948 pairs written, 92,032 held at the end). Eight workers (section 7.2): 1.9 to 112.5 MiB for the eight tables together, and no table reaches a bound. The peak resident size of a process that makes several passes grows by 2 to 9 \% in the executable (section 7.4).}

\p{\strong{A miss.} A comparison with an open side or under eager reduction costs the hook and its flag tests, at the entry and at the completion; a closed pair one write of a 16-byte cell (the stamp from which the cost of the comparison is taken) and one bit of the filter; a pair that passes the filter is named, a walk with a cache per session, and looked up. In instructions (section 7.5) the hooks alone, with nothing ever written, are 0.26 to 0.29 \% of a run; the two hooks are not on the image's list of forced inlines (\src{rlvm-machine/h10/force-inline.txt}), and whether they are calls there was not looked at. Reachability is the corpus where almost nothing is answered (5,971 closed comparisons and 627 answers with one worker): +0.06 \% of the instructions with one worker, +0.24 \% with eight; its warm pass in the guard (section 7.4) is 1.7 \% longer in the executable (0.547 against 0.538 s, CPU +0.9 \%), at the edge of what the window resolves, and unchanged on the JVM launcher (0.998). Init, where half of the lookups are answered: 0.963 in the executable, 1.005 on the JVM launcher.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.6 Both outcomes, or "true" only}

\p{gruszka keeps both. In the machine the "false" answers are next to nothing: with both kept (one worker, section 7.3) 656 of 94,567 answers on Init are "false" (0.7 \%), 4,977 of 498,657 on navier-stokes-euler (1.0 \%), 14,017 of 430,933 on cslib (3.3 \%), and the ticks move by 0.02, 0.17 and 0.17 points. And a kept "false" is the one way the table could make the machine reject a declaration that it accepted before (the driver's re-check repairs that, section 4.2, but it is a warm anomaly, and one hostile export of section 5 shows it). So the default keeps "true" answers only; \code{\verb~|-X machine.closed-pairs=false:on~} keeps both, for measurements.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.7 Eager reduction}

\p{[Eager reduction on the machine](design-eager-reduction) rests on one invariant: a declaration that defers eager comparisons runs in a session that holds no answer which could differ between the two modes, and the session flag \code{erSens} says whether it may hold one. The table keeps the invariant by three rules. Nothing is read or written under eager reduction. An answer is kept only while the flag of its session is clear at its completion: then no comparison of the session, this one included, passed a point where eager reduction takes another branch, so the answer and every session answer it used are what the machine computes in both modes. And the answers of a declaration that entered eager reduction are dropped as a whole. The price: on Init, Std and cedar more than half of the answers that qualified otherwise were not kept because their session held a possibly mode-dependent answer (46,256 of 78,698 on Init), 37 to 39 \% on con-leche and cslib, 29 \% on Mathlib, 10 \% on navier-stokes-euler; and the answers of declarations that entered eager reduction are 12 \% of the buffered ones on Mathlib, 9 \% on cslib and 1 to 4 \% elsewhere (one worker).}

\p{An experiment, \code{sens:1}, keeps such answers with a mark instead: a marked answer is not read by a run that decides the mode-dependent points itself, and an ordinary run that reads one sets its session's flag, which is what computing the answer again would have done. In counts (one worker, section 7.3) it gains on Init (24.5 \% of the ticks instead of 21.6 \%), nothing on navier-stokes-euler (12.7 instead of 12.6 \%) and loses on cslib and Mathlib (7.7 instead of 7.9 \%; 5.1 instead of 5.8 \% with the same bounds): a session that reads a marked answer must repeat a declaration that then needs eager reduction, and the repeated runs go from 854 to 1,909 on cslib and from 1,541 to 4,970 on Mathlib. It was not gated.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The patch}

\p{Three files under the package's patch directory, against the main tree of 2026-11-25 12:12 UTC, dry-run verified against the main tree of 15:32 UTC:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The switch and the table}: the new class \code{ClosedPairs} (the node table, the pair table, the names, the two hooks, the counters, the settings) and small changes in \code{Op} (the switch \code{closedpairs}, bit 30, in no default set and not in \code{all}), \code{Debug} (its name), \code{Machine} (the field \code{x6}, null while the switch is off; two calls in \code{beginDecl} and \code{reset}), \code{MachineSession} (commit at an accept, drop at any other outcome), \code{MachineEngine} and \code{MachineProvider} (the setting \code{machine.closed-pairs}, the counters), \code{Conv} (below), and one row of the settings table of \src{rlvm-machine/README.md}.}
\li{\strong{The tests}: \code{ClosedPairsTest} (14 tests, fast group), 12 further switch sets in \code{SHostileSlowTest}, 78 rows at the end of the manifest of the hostile suite.}
\li{\strong{The 78 hostile exports} (compressed, binary): a tar file with paths from the repository root.}
}

\p{\strong{Call sites in the conversion code}, for the integrator who merges this with the change of the session tables' layout of package OPT-D: \code{Conv} is touched in three places and nowhere else. (1) The last line of \code{convForce} calls the new private method \code{convEnter} instead of \code{convLoop}. (2) The last line of \code{k_conv2} does the same. \code{convEnter} tests the switch, calls \code{ClosedPairs.enter} and falls through to \code{convLoop}. (3) \code{rec} starts with a test of the switch and \code{ClosedPairs.leave}. The table is a structure of its own: it reads values and runs only through the accessors \code{Values.kind / flags / aux / a / b / c / memo} and \code{Runs.len / arg / rest}, writes the session's pair table only through \code{Pairs.add}, and uses \code{Hash.cell}; it depends on no layout constant of the intern tables or the pair table. It also reads \code{m.decl}, \code{m.declStamp}, \code{m.ticks()}, \code{m.ER}, \code{m.erSens}, \code{m.erAware}, \code{m.erSeen}, \code{m.dbgNest}, \code{m.levels} and \code{m.lists}. With the switch off the cost is two tests of \code{m.sw} per comparison that reaches the pass loop, and the counters are those of the base (section 6).}

\p{\strong{Settings.} \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=default,closedpairs~} turns it on. \code{\verb~|-X machine.closed-pairs=KEY:VALUE,..~}: \code{min:N} (4), \code{false:on|off} (off), \code{nodes:LOG2} (20), \code{pairs:LOG2} (19), \code{fuel:N} (256), \code{decl:N} (16,384 answers buffered per declaration), \code{filter:on|off} (on), \code{sens:0|1} (0), \code{shadow:on|off} (off: the table is written and nothing is read). Statistics: \code{machine.closedPairs.*} (34 counters, summed over the workers).}

\p{\strong{For the switch list.} [The switch reference](design-switches-document) needs a row for the switch when the patch is applied: name \code{closedpairs}, bit 30, owner OPT-H, state "experiment, off", gate and measurement this report.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. Soundness: the sentences of the task, checked against the code}

\subtree{
\title{4.1 A kept "true" is a derivable equality of closed terms}

\ul{
\li{\strong{It was derived.} An entry is written only by \code{Conv.rec} with the answer T of the comparison of the pair itself: the machine's procedure, with the shortcuts of its switch set, completed on it. The machine's "true" answers are derivable under the precondition that both sides are well-typed, which its inference establishes ([the proof-comparison shortcuts](design-proof-comparison-shortcuts)); the declaration that wrote the entry was accepted.}
\li{\strong{It is about closed terms.} Both values carry no flag \code{OPEN} when the entry is written and when it is read, and the name walk fails on anything that is open, on a let variable (they are flagged \code{OPEN}) and on a typed binding (a suspension with a non-zero aux field, which a frame of a closure can hold: its type is the declared domain, not the type of its value). A name mentions no session id: section 2.1. So equal names are equal closed terms up to evaluation, and the fact "these two closed terms are definitionally equal" does not depend on the declaration being checked, as long as the environment only grows.}
\li{\strong{The environment only grows for the reader.} The rank rule of section 2.4: an entry is read by declarations of higher rank only. The reader's two values exist, so their constants are visible to it; everything the derivation unfolded lies below them.}
\li{\strong{Universe parameters.} A level parameter of the enclosing declaration is in the name by its name id, an instantiated one by the structure of its level, a closure by its substitution. A "true" for terms with a parameter \em{u} is schematic in \em{u} and is read only for the same terms with the same parameter name. Hostile rows of family C, seeded faults 1, 2 and 9.}
\li{\strong{Literals.} Small and two-word Nat literals are named by value; a stored big number and a string have no name. Naming a stored number by its store id would be a wrong equality: the first number a session computes has the same id in every session (hostile row \code{oh-lit-computed-big-bad}, seeded fault 7, accepted with the fault).}
\li{\strong{Proof irrelevance and types.} The procedure decides proof irrelevance from the types it infers for the two values; for closed values these are functions of the values. No typing context exists outside a closed value.}
\li{\strong{No transitivity.} The table holds exact pairs, like \code{POS}; two kept pairs never answer a third (hostile row \code{oh-kept-true-non-transitive-bad}: rejected in every configuration).}
}

\p{So a kept "true" can never make the machine accept a statement that is not derivable. It can make the machine accept where the official kernel rejects a derivable statement: the official caches live for one declaration and its answer for a pair depends on what it reduced before ([the divergence classes](design-official-divergence), classes 2 and 3). That is the tolerated kind (d) of [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract), and it is the class [warm sessions](design-warm-sessions) are in already: of the eleven rows of section 5 that the machine with the table accepts against the official verdict, ten are accepted by the default set of today as well (eight through its warm session, two through its shortcuts under eager reduction); the eleventh is new (below).}

\p{\strong{One thing a kept "true" does that a computed one does not.} An answer from the table skips the reductions of the comparison, and with them the memos they would have written. The official kernel's answer for a later pair can depend on such a memo (its cache of full reductions is read by cheap ones). Row \code{oh-kept-false-then-false-bad}: the first declaration compares \code{\verb~|a = 5~} and then, inside a same-head comparison, \code{\verb~|a = b~}, which is false after the first; the second declaration does the same two comparisons and is rejected officially and by every configuration without the table. With the table the first comparison of the second declaration is answered from it, the full reduction of \code{a} never runs, and \code{\verb~|a = b~} is true in the empty cache: accepted. The statement is \code{\verb~|True /\ True~} and derivable, and the twin with the two sub-proofs in the other order is accepted officially; kind T. A warm session has the same effect in the other direction (it keeps the memo), which is why the default set of today rejects this row.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.2 A kept "false" can only produce a reject, and every reject is re-checked}

\p{With the default setting no "false" is kept, so this concerns \code{false:on} only. A "false" answer of a comparison is never positive evidence in the kernel's algorithm: every acceptance rests on "true" answers. A kept "false" can therefore end in a REJECT of the machine, in a detour (lazy delta unfolds where the arguments would have been found equal), or, through the detour, in more work and a decline. The driver's protocol for the machine's outcomes (\code{Worker.check}, \code{resolve}, \code{rejected}): a REJECT of the warm lane is first checked by the strict machine engine, which has no switch and a fresh session and never sees the table (an accept there is counted as a warm anomaly and is the verdict), then by the reference engine; a DECLINE is retried cold on the same machine and then decided by the reference engine. A rejection is final without the re-check only under \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck~}, a measuring option. A unit above the first failure is abandoned, and a non-accept of a unit that started while the file was loading is run again after the load. So a reject caused by a kept "false" becomes the official verdict. Row \code{oh-kept-false-reused-ok}: with \code{false:on} the machine rejects and the pipeline accepts, as the official checker does; with the default it accepts by itself.}

\p{\strong{Can the table turn an accept into a decline?} It can change the machine's own outcome in both directions, and the contract does not rest on the machine's outcome. A "true" from the table replaces a computation whose answer is "true" (the same result with less work: con-leche has fewer budget declines, section 7), or a computation whose answer would have been "false" in the present cache state (then the machine accepts more). But the computation it skips also wrote memos and spent or earned probe budget, so what the machine does \em{after} the hit is not the base's run with one step removed: a later speculative segment can be cut at another place, a later comparison can take another route, and a declaration that the base accepted could in principle be declined, or one that entered eager reduction after a failure could be restarted at another point (Mathlib, one worker: 1,549 restarts for eager reduction instead of 1,502, the same 120 units declined). None of this reaches the verdict: a DECLINE of the machine is retried cold and then decided by the reference engine, a REJECT is re-checked as above, and an ACCEPT rests on "true" answers that are derivable. What it can cost is time, and the evidence that it does not cost verdicts on real inputs is the gates of section 6: the official verdict on the eight corpora with 8 and 4 workers and both launchers with the same number of declined units as the base, the 230 tests, 17,775 mutants, the invariance suite and the differential engine, with the table on.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.3 What survives, and what must not}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Budgets.} An entry is written by the frame that completes the comparison. A segment that is cut removes its frames (\code{Frames.k_try}), and a segment whose budget was capped by the enclosing one passes the cut on; a comparison that completed inside a segment before the cut has an answer that does not depend on the budget and is kept, as its \code{POS} is.}
\li{\strong{Failed declarations.} The buffer is committed in \code{MachineSession.check0} only on the path that returns ACCEPT, and dropped on every other path and at the next \code{beginDecl} (a restart for eager reduction, an exception).}
\li{\strong{Passes and stores.} The table belongs to the \code{Machine} of a \code{MachineSession}; the driver makes new sessions for every pass and every store, also when it keeps the engines. Rows, term ids and name ids of another store never meet it.}
\li{\strong{The strict engine.} It runs with no switch; the field is never allocated there.}
\li{\strong{Debug modes.} Under \code{memocheck} every answer taken from the table is computed again by the machine with the table bypassed and must be the same, else the unit is declined with an audit failure. This is a lane of the machine's gate.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Hostile exports and seeded faults}

\p{78 exports, written for this switch with the builder of the hostile suite and the gadgets of [the review of the default set](rep-soundness-default-set) (the box \code{\verb~|a = (f intro).1~}, \code{\verb~|b = (f h).1~}, whose comparison is true in an empty official cache and false once the full reduction of \code{a} was cached). Every official verdict was taken from leanchecker 4.36.0 in the project's Docker image, as the suite does; the expected verdict of the generator was right on all 78: 41 accept, 37 reject. They are offered as rows of the suite with their marks; 11 carry the mark of the tolerated kind, each with a certificate that the official checker accepts (the twin with the two sub-proofs in the other order, or the plain form of the statement).}

\table{
\tr{\th{family}\th{rows}\th{what it aims at}\th{official}\th{result}}
\tr{\td{A: a kept "true"}\td{10}\td{a closed pair compared true in an accepted declaration and needed later, after the official cache was filled: directly, after twelve other declarations, as arguments of another head, flipped; and that two kept pairs never answer a third (\code{\verb~|d intro = d h~}, \code{\verb~|d intro = 5~}, then \code{\verb~|d h = 5~})}\td{6 reject, 4 accept}\td{4 rows of kind T: accepted where the table is what a later declaration reads, and by the default set of today through its warm session; the transitivity row is rejected everywhere}}
\tr{\td{B: a kept "false"}\td{7}\td{a closed pair that completes with "false" inside an accepted declaration (arguments of a regular definition that then unfolds) and is true officially later; the same pair needed false later; a "false" that must stay one}\td{2 reject, 5 accept}\td{with \code{false:on} the machine rejects two official accepts and the re-check repairs them (note \code{recheck}); with the default nothing is rejected, and one row of kind T is accepted (section 4.1)}}
\tr{\td{C: universe levels}\td{9}\td{\code{\verb~|A.{0} = B~} kept, \code{\verb~|A.{1} = B~} false; a parameter by name: \code{\verb~|A.{u} = C.{u}~} kept, \code{\verb~|A.{u} = C.{v}~} and \code{\verb~|A.{u} = C.{0}~} false; the same inside a closure over one shared term under two substitutions}\td{6 reject, 3 accept}\td{the official verdict everywhere: all strict}}
\tr{\td{D: literals}\td{27}\td{an immediate, the two words of a two-word literal, the edges 2^31 and 2^63, stored big literals, a string, successors of a non-literal, Nat folding, and a number the session computes (the predecessor of a big major premise), whose store id every session issues again}\td{14 reject, 13 accept}\td{the official verdict everywhere: all strict}}
\tr{\td{E: eager reduction}\td{10}\td{a closed pair of lambdas whose body comparison depends on the mode (\code{\verb~|fun p => X~} against \code{\verb~|fun p => Y p~} of the family \code{\verb~|X = Y p~}): plain then eager, eager alone, in plain mode inside a declaration that enters eager reduction, both modes in one declaration; and a closed pair that is the same in both modes: reached first through an eager comparison and then in plain mode, plain first and then under eager reduction, under eager reduction only}\td{2 reject, 8 accept}\td{the two rejects are accepted by the default set of today \em{without} the table: the known tolerated family under one more binder (kind T by the family argument of the review, section 10.1); the table changes no verdict of the family}}
\tr{\td{F: shapes of the key}\td{15}\td{two closed lambdas, a suspended argument, a let in the later declaration, two proofs, two propositions, the captured value of a closure, the field of a stuck projection as a term and as a forced value}\td{7 reject, 8 accept}\td{4 rows of kind T (the box under each shape); the strict ones get the official verdict}}
}

\p{\strong{Verdicts by configuration}, JVM launcher of the final build, the machine as primary engine, every row with the official verdict beside it. "Accept against the official verdict" counts kind T rows only: no strict row was accepted by any configuration, and the reference engine gives the official verdict on all 78.}

\table{
\tr{\th{configuration}\th{accepts against the official verdict (all kind T)}\th{rejections of the machine that the re-check repairs}\th{read from the table}}
\tr{\td{the default set (warm sessions), no table}\td{10: the eight box rows of A and F, the two eager rows}\td{2 (\code{oh-kept-false-reused-ok}, \code{oh-kept-false-then-true-ok}: the session's memo of the full reduction; class 3 of the divergence classes)}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{the default set with the table}\td{10, the same}\td{2, the same}\td{in 1 row: a file of the suite is one session, and the session's pair table answers first}}
\tr{\td{the default set without \code{warm} and \code{pchk}, no table}\td{2: the eager rows}\td{0}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{the same with the table (a session per declaration: the table is the only memory)}\td{11: the ten and \code{oh-kept-false-then-false-bad}}\td{0}\td{in 42 rows}}
\tr{\td{the same with \code{false:on}}\td{10 (the eleventh is rejected: its "false" is kept)}\td{2 (\code{oh-kept-false-reused-ok}, \code{oh-kept-false-then-true-ok})}\td{in 44 rows}}
\tr{\td{the table alone on the generic tier (\code{closedpairs})}\td{9 (the eight rows that enter eager reduction are declined by the machine and decided by the reference engine)}\td{0}\td{in 54 rows}}
