\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{144.22 M}\td{126.02 M}\td{-12.6 \%}\td{6.22 M}\td{4.20 M}\td{-32.5 \%}\td{-12.3 \%}\td{-37.4 \%}\td{1,632,576}\td{949,177}\td{494,748}\td{19.17 M}\td{182,661}\td{869,134}\td{32.1 MiB}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{215.96 M}\td{198.93 M}\td{-7.9 \%}\td{9.29 M}\td{7.53 M}\td{-18.9 \%}\td{-6.6 \%}\td{-17.3 \%}\td{2,307,705}\td{690,144}\td{417,076}\td{12.73 M}\td{116,987}\td{537,403}\td{28.1 MiB}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{574.10 M}\td{542.85 M}\td{-5.4 \%}\td{24.57 M}\td{21.31 M}\td{-13.3 \%}\td{-5.1 \%}\td{-13.1 \%}\td{7,100,004}\td{1,677,968}\td{964,604}\td{29.55 M}\td{92,032}\td{569,827}\td{28.1 MiB}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Unfoldings} go down by a third and more on Init, Std, cedar and navier-stokes-euler (33 to 41 \%), by 28 \% on con-leche, 19 \% on cslib and 13 \% on Mathlib; the ticks by 5.4 to 21.6 \%, the records by 5 to 18 \%, the reductions to weak head normal form past their memo by 13 to 45 \%. With fewer records there are fewer sessions (Init: 672 instead of 796; Mathlib: 14,245 instead of 14,838).}
\li{\strong{Against the estimate} of [the report of package OPT-E](rep-OPT-E), "a third of the unfoldings": met on four corpora, not on the two largest. gruszka's table removes 23 \% of gruszka's unfoldings on Mathlib and 35 \% on cslib; this one removes 13 and 19 \% of the machine's, with the mode rule of section 2.7 holding back 29 and 37 \% of the answers there.}
\li{\strong{What is answered.} A kept pair is answered 2.7 to 3.6 times (1.3 times on con-leche). An answered comparison had cost 31 to 54 ticks when it was computed: these are short comparisons, many of them. The run saves more ticks than that sum on six corpora (Init: 7.4 M less against 4.8 M); presumably the first computation ran in a session that already held part of its work, and a later session would not.}
\li{\strong{The filter} lets 24 \% of the closed pairs through on Mathlib, 30 \% on cslib, 52 to 72 \% on Init, Std, cedar, reachability and navier-stokes-euler, 89 \% on con-leche; of those, 50 to 60 \% are answered (36 \% on con-leche, 16 \% on reachability).}
\li{\strong{Reachability} has 5,971 closed comparisons in the whole file and 627 answers: nothing to gain, and the corpus that shows what a miss costs (sections 7.4 and 7.5).}
\li{\strong{Declines and eager reduction.} The machine declines the same units with and without the table on seven corpora; on con-leche one of the two units that the base declines is accepted with the table. Runs repeated for eager reduction: 107 and 109 on Init, 133 and 140 on Std, 235 and 231 on cedar, 23 and 26 on con-leche, 43 and 43 on navier-stokes-euler, 812 and 854 on cslib, 1,502 and 1,549 on Mathlib: a kept answer can move the point where a declaration first needs the eager mode.}
\li{\strong{Mathlib} reaches the bound of 2^20 nodes once with one worker: 303,948 pairs were written and 92,032 are held at the end. Section 7.3 has the run with larger bounds.}
}
}

