\ul{
\li{\strong{The threshold.} One tick instead of four keeps 5 \% more pairs on Init, 16 \% more on cslib and 52 \% more on navier-stokes-euler for 0.02 to 0.16 points of the ticks; 16 ticks lose 0.7 to 0.9 points, 64 ticks 2.1 to 3.6. Four is the default.}
\li{\strong{"False" answers} are 0.7 to 3.3 \% of the answers and 0.02 to 0.17 points of the ticks. Not kept by default (section 2.6).}
\li{\strong{\code{sens:1}} helps the small corpus and hurts the large ones, through the declarations that are run again for eager reduction. Not a candidate as it is.}
\li{\strong{The bounds.} Mathlib with one worker and bounds eight times larger: no restart of the table, 254,179 pairs and 1.3 million nodes in 56 MiB, 5.8 \% of the ticks instead of 5.4 \%. With eight workers the default bounds are not reached.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7.4 Time: the guard}

\p{One quiet window (\src{bench/quiet.py}: the 8 measurement cores of the aarch64 development host reserved, the other cores busy with the gates of this and three other packages), 2026-11-25, 14:53 to 15:28 UTC: first the executable (the base, the candidate with the switch off, the candidate with the switch on), then the JVM launcher with its default heap for the machine, \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~} (the base, the candidate with the switch on). Per corpus the variants were interleaved round by round, three rounds; a process checks the export several times (\code{\verb~|-X passes=N~}: 8 passes for Init and reachability, 6 for Std, cedar and con-leche, 4 for navier-stokes-euler and cslib, 3 for Mathlib in the executable; 10, 8, 6 and 4 on the JVM launcher) with 8 workers and the load pipelined with checking. A cell is the mean over the rounds of the median warm pass of a process (the executable: passes 2 and later; the JVM launcher: the second half of the passes), or of the mean warm pass where that median is below 0.5 s, by [the rule of the guard](dec-YP0N). CPU is the process CPU time of the same passes.}

\p{\strong{The executable.}}

