\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{3 x 8}\td{1.267}\td{1.261}\td{0.996}\td{8.48}\td{8.57}\td{1.011}\td{0.986 1.004 1.043}\td{1,838}\td{1,835}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{3 x 6}\td{3.476}\td{3.474}\td{1.000}\td{25.64}\td{25.85}\td{1.008}\td{0.963 1.026 1.036}\td{2,277}\td{2,351}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{3 x 6}\td{5.017}\td{4.621}\td{0.921}\td{33.89}\td{34.20}\td{1.009}\td{1.009 1.032 0.987}\td{2,846}\td{2,861}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{3 x 4}\td{14.201}\td{12.978}\td{0.914}\td{97.60}\td{96.95}\td{0.993}\td{1.004 0.964 1.013}\td{5,074}\td{5,213}}
\tr{\td{\strong{geometric mean}}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{\strong{0.976}}\td{}\td{}\td{\strong{0.997}}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{What the window resolves.} The candidate with the switch off computes what the base computes (section 6), so it is a second sample of the same program: against the base it is between 0.969 (Mathlib) and 1.027 (con-leche) in the pass and between 0.986 and 1.017 in CPU, geometric means 1.001 and 0.999. One window of three rounds on this host, on this day, resolves about 3 \% for one corpus and a few tenths of a percent for the geometric mean. The base's pass of Mathlib is 14.7 s here; on the quiet host of the wave's baseline it was 13.1 s.}
\li{\strong{The switch on, the executable}: geometric mean 0.994 in the pass and 0.991 in CPU; no corpus more than 1.7 \% slower. Beyond the resolution: the CPU of Init (0.974), Std (0.981), cedar (0.988) and con-leche (0.986), every round on the same side, and the pass of Init (0.963). Not beyond it: navier-stokes-euler (CPU 0.996), cslib (1.002) and Mathlib (0.993), where 9.8, 5.8 and 4.5 \% of the ticks are gone (section 7.2); and reachability (1.009 in CPU, 1.017 in the pass, which is 9 ms).}
\li{\strong{The switch on, the JVM launcher}: geometric mean 0.976 in the pass and 0.997 in CPU; no corpus more than 0.5 \% slower. The pass ratios of cslib (0.921) and Mathlib (0.914) come from single slow passes of the base (17.7 s among passes of 12.5 to 14.1 s on Mathlib, 6.0 s among 4.4 to 5.4 s on cslib, with the CPU of the others); in CPU the two are 1.009 and 0.993. No regression, and no gain that the window resolves; this launcher had no second sample of the base.}
\li{\strong{Peak resident size}, a process of 3 to 10 passes: the executable 2 to 9 \% more with the switch on (Init +26 MiB, Std +29, cedar +65, reachability +12, con-leche +45, navier-stokes-euler +127, Mathlib +192; cslib +204, where the build with the switch off shows +161 in the same window); the JVM launcher 0 to 3 \% (Mathlib +139 MiB). The tables of a pass become garbage with its sessions. A process that makes one pass shows no growth (Mathlib, 8 workers, one run each: 4,004 MiB with the switch off, 3,976 MiB with it on).}
}
}
\subtree{
\title{7.5 Instructions}

\p{User-space instructions of one run of the executable from start to exit (the load and one pass, nothing warm), counted with \code{\verb~|perf stat -e instructions:u~} by \src{bench/measure.sh} in the project's measurement container on the aarch64 development host; the counters covered the whole run in every case. A count, not a time, so it needs no quiet window; it is the quantity the arena ranks by, there on x86-64. One run per cell; a second run of the same command differs by 0.003 \% (Mathlib, 8 workers, the base), by 0.08 \% (Mathlib, 8 workers, the switch on: the schedule decides which worker keeps what) and by 5 thousand instructions of 419 billion (navier-stokes-euler, one worker, the base).}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{workers}\th{base}\th{switch off}\th{against the base}\th{switch on}\th{against the base}\th{ticks (sections 7.1 and 7.2)}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{1}\td{70.27 G}\td{70.30 G}\td{+0.05 \%}\td{62.86 G}\td{-10.53 \%}\td{-21.6 \%}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{1}\td{121.32 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{111.39 G}\td{-8.18 \%}\td{-17.7 \%}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{1}\td{171.69 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{161.21 G}\td{-6.10 \%}\td{-14.3 \%}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{1}\td{15.01 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{15.02 G}\td{+0.06 \%}\td{-0.2 \%}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{1}\td{154.87 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{144.61 G}\td{-6.63 \%}\td{-13.9 \%}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{1}\td{418.84 G}\td{419.08 G}\td{+0.06 \%}\td{399.51 G}\td{-4.62 \%}\td{-12.6 \%}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{1}\td{498.20 G}\td{498.45 G}\td{+0.05 \%}\td{483.06 G}\td{-3.04 \%}\td{-7.9 \%}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{1}\td{1459.53 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{1433.78 G}\td{-1.76 \%}\td{-5.4 \%}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{8}\td{70.35 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{65.63 G}\td{-6.71 \%}\td{-15.1 \%}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{8}\td{121.71 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{114.72 G}\td{-5.74 \%}\td{-12.5 \%}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{8}\td{172.19 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{164.96 G}\td{-4.20 \%}\td{-10.1 \%}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{8}\td{15.07 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{15.11 G}\td{+0.24 \%}\td{+0.1 \%}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{8}\td{155.50 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{148.29 G}\td{-4.64 \%}\td{-10.1 \%}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{8}\td{420.03 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{405.64 G}\td{-3.43 \%}\td{-9.8 \%}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{8}\td{499.02 G}\td{-}\td{-}\td{489.10 G}\td{-1.99 \%}\td{-5.8 \%}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{8}\td{1462.85 G}\td{1463.30 G}\td{+0.03 \%}\td{1444.00 G}\td{-1.29 \%}\td{-4.5 \%}}
}

\p{\strong{Where the instructions go}, one worker, by three settings that take the table apart: nothing is ever written (a threshold that no comparison reaches: the two tests and the cost stamp of every closed comparison are all that runs); the table is written and never read (\code{shadow:on}: the names of the answers that are kept, the buffer, the commit); and the filter off (every closed pair is named and looked up).}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus, one worker}\th{base}\th{switch off}\th{on, nothing ever written}\th{on, written and never read}\th{on (default settings)}\th{on, the filter off}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{418.84 G}\td{419.08 G (+0.06 \%)}\td{420.06 G (+0.29 \%)}\td{423.21 G (+1.04 \%)}\td{399.51 G (-4.62 \%)}\td{399.53 G (-4.61 \%)}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{70.27 G}\td{70.30 G (+0.05 \%)}\td{70.45 G (+0.26 \%)}\td{70.74 G (+0.67 \%)}\td{62.86 G (-10.53 \%)}\td{62.69 G (-10.78 \%)}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The switch off} costs 0.03 to 0.06 \% of the instructions: the two tests of the switch and the larger code.}
\li{\strong{The switch on}: 1.3 \% (Mathlib) to 6.7 \% (Init) fewer instructions with eight workers, 1.8 to 10.5 \% fewer with one; nothing on reachability (+0.1 to +0.2 \%). That is 0.29 to 0.49 of the reduction in ticks; a run's instructions include the load, which no tick counts.}
\li{\strong{Against the time} of section 7.4, eight workers: Init 6.7 \% of the instructions and 2.6 \% of the CPU; Std 5.7 and 1.9; cedar 4.2 and 1.2; con-leche 4.6 and 1.4; navier-stokes-euler 3.4 and 0.4; cslib 2.0 and none; Mathlib 1.3 and 0.7, the last three inside what the window resolves (the instructions of a run with one cold pass, the CPU of warm passes, both with the load). The instructions the table removes are cheaper than the ones it adds. The likely reason is that its own work is probes of a node table and a pair table that are not in the processor's caches, while the comparisons it answers are short and ran on values the session had just touched; no profile was made, so this is a reading of the counts, not a measurement.}
\li{\strong{The parts}: the hooks with nothing written +0.26 to +0.29 \%; writing adds 0.4 points on Init and 0.75 on navier-stokes-euler (Mathlib with eight workers: the last item); reading, net of its lookups, is then 5.7 \% of the instructions on navier-stokes-euler, 11.2 \% on Init and 1.8 \% on Mathlib with eight workers. The filter saves no instructions: without it navier-stokes-euler is the same and Init 0.25 points better.}
\li{Mathlib, 8 workers, the same three settings: base 1,462.85 G; nothing written 1,466.59 G (+0.26 \%); written and never read 1,470.40 G (+0.52 \%); on 1,444.00 G (-1.29 \%; the second run 1,442.87 G, -1.36 \%).}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. What it would take to make it a default, and what an adversarial review still has to do}

\p{The switch is delivered off. By [the rule for a default](dec-QN1O) it can enter a default set only after an adversarial pass by an agent that does not own the code, with hostile exports judged by the official checker, seeded faults, the rows in the suite and a list of what the pass did not establish; and by [the rule of the guard](dec-YP0N) only if the geometric mean of the eight warm passes goes down and no corpus is more than 3 \% slower, on the executable, without a loss on the JVM launcher. What this package did is the owner's part: the gates, 78 hostile rows of its own, 16 seeded faults. What is left, in the order in which it should be done:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The guard.} The letter of the guard is met and its purpose is not. The executable: geometric mean of the warm passes 0.994, no corpus more than 1.7 \% slower; the JVM launcher: 0.976, no corpus more than 0.5 \% slower (section 7.4). But the same window puts a build that computes exactly what the base computes at 1.001, with single corpora between 0.969 and 1.027, so 0.994 is not a measured gain; and on navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib, the corpora that decide the metric, the CPU of the pass does not move (0.996, 1.002, 0.993) although 4.5 to 9.8 \% of the ticks and 1.3 to 3.4 \% of the instructions are gone. What the window does resolve is 1 to 3 \% of the CPU on Init, Std, cedar and con-leche. A default needs a gain in time on the large corpora first. More rounds would say whether there is one of a few tenths of a percent; the list at the end of this section is about making one.}
\li{\strong{An attack on the names by somebody else.} The soundness of a "true" from the table rests on one property: two values with one name are definitionally equal. The seeded faults of section 5 show that the rows reach the level list of a constant, the substitution of a closure, the parameter name, the words of a literal, the successor count, the frame slots, the arguments and the field of a projection. Not attacked by any row: the type of a lambda (the kind \code{PII}) against the Pi closure over the same term; placeholder slots (two frames that differ only in which slots are placeholders); a term of the generated region; a far frame that the walk refuses (that it refuses \em{every} far frame); a level whose structure differs from an equivalent one (a miss only, by the argument); a suspension that is forced between the lookup and the write of one comparison (its name changes: a miss only). A reviewer should also try to make the walk name something that is not closed: the flag \code{OPEN} of a value built by a code block, a typed binding inside a frame, a let variable whose flag was lost.}
\li{\strong{The rank rule under real schedules.} A file is read by the gates in file order by one worker, or by several workers whose tables are private. No test puts an entry of a later declaration in front of an earlier one except the unit test. With a shared table (below) the rule would carry the argument alone.}
\li{\strong{Eager reduction.} The mode rule keeps an answer only while the session's flag is clear. The two shortcuts answer some comparisons before the points that set the flag are reached (section 5, family E: the table then holds an answer that the \em{machine} gives in both modes and the official kernel does not; the base accepts those rows already). A review should look for a pair that the machine itself answers differently in the two modes while the flag stays clear: the K rule forms of the eager family, a probe of the plain phase that was cut. The setting \code{sens:1} must not be turned on without that review.}
\li{\strong{The skipped memo} of section 4.1 is a new way to an accept of the tolerated kind. One row shows it. A review should decide whether the family is covered by the certificates of the review of the default set (the twin order) or needs rows of its own, and look for a reduction whose \em{absence} lets an inference step pass that is not a comparison.}
\li{\strong{The differential engine and the corpora} with the switch on by default: every gate of section 6 again on the distribution in which the switch is in \code{\verb~|Op.SW_DEFAULT~}, the arena's own procedure, and the profile of the optional profile-guided build.}
\li{\strong{Memory.} The peak resident size of a process that makes several passes grows by 2 to 9 \% in the executable (Mathlib: 4,617 against 4,425 MiB) and by 0 to 3 \% on the JVM launcher. The bounds were set without a budget: at its hard limit a worker's table is about 64 MiB, half a GiB for eight workers; measured on Mathlib with eight workers: 112.5 MiB. A default needs the bound stated as a budget and a decision what happens at it; today the table starts again from empty, which one worker on Mathlib does once.}
}

\p{\strong{What could make it worth more}, none of it measured in time. (1) \em{Find where the time goes.} The table removes 4.5 to 15 \% of the ticks with eight workers and far less of the time (section 7.4); in instructions it removes a third to a half of what it removes in ticks (section 7.5). The answered comparisons are short, 31 to 54 ticks each, and an answer costs two walks for the names and probes of a node table and a pair table of up to 14 MiB per worker (Mathlib) that no other code touches. A profile of the image with the switch on (the name walk, the two probes, the misses of the per-session cache of names) is the first step; this package made none. Candidates after it: the name of a value kept in the value's record instead of a cache of 4,096 cells; a threshold on the cost of the pair when it is \em{read}, by head pair (the filter could count what a head pair saved); no lookup at all for head pairs whose answers were cheap. (2) \em{One table per engine} instead of one per worker: section 2.5 has what it could add at most (1 to 6.5 points of the ticks) and what it needs. (3) \em{The mode rule} holds back a third to three fifths of the candidate answers on Init, Std, cedar, con-leche and cslib; \code{sens:1} is not the way to get them (section 7.3). (4) The answers of declarations that enter eager reduction are dropped although they were written in plain mode before any eager comparison (the deferred comparisons run last): 12 \% of the buffered answers on Mathlib. (5) Larger bounds matter only for one worker on Mathlib (section 7.3).}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Not measured, not established}

\ul{
\li{x86-64, the arena's architecture: every time and every instruction count here is from the aarch64 development host. The arena's own procedure was not run.}
\li{A profile: where the time of a lookup goes was not measured. A shared table. The settings \code{sens:1}, \code{false:on}, the thresholds and the bounds in time (counts only); \code{sens:1} under the gates.}
\li{The switch together with the change of the session tables' layout of package OPT-D and with the other levers of this wave (the probe budget of \code{eager}, huge pages, the loader): each was measured against its own base.}
\li{The session cap with the table on: the cap of 1 MiB was chosen without it, and a table that outlives the session could move the best cap.}
\li{Soundness is argued and tested, not proved: the same standing as every switch of the default set ([what is left open](prob-HC9J)).}
\li{The guard is one window of three rounds on a host whose other cores ran gates; it was not repeated, and the JVM launcher had no second sample of the base. The 1.7 \% of reachability in the executable is not explained by the counts (5,971 closed comparisons in the file).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10. Tools}

\p{All outside the repository: private copies of the tracked sources (a pristine base, the candidate, a copy with the 16 seeded faults behind a system property) with their own Maven repositories and distributions; the generator of the 78 hostile exports on the builder of the hostile suite and the library of the review of the default set, with a judge that runs the official checker in Docker, the reference engine and the machine under named switch sets through the launcher and prints what the table wrote and answered per row; the count runs (one worker, the statistics of the launcher) and their table generator; the interleaved guard window on the tool of package OPT-D; the instruction counts through \src{bench/measure.sh} in the measurement container; the patch script, which diffs the candidate against the pristine base and tests every patch against the main tree. To repeat the counts: \code{\verb~|rlvm --threads 1 --stats -X stats-file=F -X machine.switches=default,closedpairs FILE~} and read \code{machine.closedPairs.*}.}
}
